# Valgrind suppressions for the constant-time harness (ct_flows, ct.yml).
#
# Policy (see SECURITY_TESTING.md, "Constant-time verification"): a suppression is allowed only
# for a KNOWN, ACCEPTED pattern inside a dependency that we cannot fix at our
# boundary, and every entry must carry a justification. Anything not listed
# here fails the run. Both families below are P-256-only; the
# curve25519-dalek paths run without any suppression.
#
# Verified against curve25519-dalek 4.1.3 / p256 0.13.2 / elliptic-curve
# 0.13.8 / sec1 0.7.3 / subtle 2.6.1 (Valgrind 3.24, aarch64 + x86_64 Linux,
# debug and release), 2026-07-06; re-triaged on CI 2026-07-10 (rustc 1.97.0,
# Valgrind 3.22, x86_64) after a compiler bump changed release inlining.
# The workspace has since moved to curve25519-dalek 5.0 / p256 0.14 /
# elliptic-curve 0.14 / sec1 0.8; in 0.6.1 the compressed-point family was
# removed rather than re-anchored (see Family 2). Re-triage on the next run
# that reports a miss.
# Re-triage when bumping these crates or on suppression misses after a
# toolchain drift (ct.yml runs unpinned @stable by design, to catch codegen
# that turns constant-time source leaky — so anchors must be drift-robust:
# prefer a stable, uniquely-named frame over one whose match depends on
# demangled generic-argument ordering).
# Each family is listed for the error kinds observed or plausible across
# codegen (Cond for flag-based branches, Value4/Value8 for tag-indexed
# dispatch), same justification.
#
# Family 1: p256 hash-to-curve internals branch on a subtle::CtOption
# discriminant that is invariant-true by construction. In p256 / primeorder
# 0.14 the one such assert left is `tv4.invert().unwrap()` in the OSSWU map
# (primeorder 0.14.0, src/osswu.rs:146): tv4 = A * CMOV(Z, -tv2, tv2 != 0)
# is never zero for P-256 (A = -3, Z = -10), so the inversion always
# succeeds, yet `CtOption::unwrap` still executes a hard `assert!` on that
# (tainted) always-true flag. (Under p256 0.13 it was the affine
# `decompress(..).unwrap()` in map_to_curve.) The condition never varies
# with the secret input, so no timing signal exists; the taint is by design
# (the hash-to-curve input is the password, or the private input of an
# RFC 9497 OPRF / VOPRF / POPRF). Dependency-internal; cannot be declassified
# at our boundary.
# The glob covers more than that assert: the leading `...` suppresses every
# error beneath `hash_from_bytes`, as far down the captured stack as
# `--num-callers` reaches (12 return addresses by default, inlined frames on
# top) — the OSSWU selects,
# `sqrt_ratio_3mod4`, the 48-byte field reduction and the field inversion
# itself. The same inversion code (crypto-bigint's safegcd) also runs
# unsuppressed on secret points in every compressed P-256 encoding (see
# Family 2), so a leak in it shows there too, unless inlining compiles it
# differently at the two sites; the others run tainted only here and are
# constant-time by source reading.
# A narrower anchor (an `unwrap` frame first) is not used, for the reason
# below: release inlining folds the unwrap into `hash_from_bytes`.
# Anchored on `hash_from_bytes` (the hash-to-curve entry of the `hash2curve`
# crate, which p256 0.14 re-exports) rather than the earlier
# `*hash2curve*hash_from_bytes*`:
# release inlining collapses osswu/map_to_curve/unwrap and names the inlined
# frame, by its DWARF name, `hash_from_bytes<...::hash2curve::...>`, where
# "hash2curve" no longer precedes the name — so the ordered pattern missed
# under rustc 1.97.
{
   p256-hash2curve-invariant-ctoption-asserts-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:*hash_from_bytes*
}
{
   p256-hash2curve-invariant-ctoption-asserts-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   ...
   fun:*hash_from_bytes*
}
{
   p256-hash2curve-invariant-ctoption-asserts-value4
   Memcheck:Value4
   ...
   fun:*hash_from_bytes*
}

# Family 2: SEC1 *uncompressed* serialization in `P256Group::to_bytes`
# (CPace, SPAKE2, SPAKE2+), which also encodes secret points such as the
# shared K. `to_sec1_point(false)` never runs `Tag::compress_y`; it
# constant-time-selects between the 0x04 encoding and the identity encoding
# on `is_identity()`, and sec1 then dispatches on that tag byte
# (`Tag::from_u8` via `EncodedPoint::tag/len/as_bytes`). The dispatched value
# therefore reveals only whether the point is the identity — and every caller
# that encodes a secret point has already aborted on the identity, a public
# protocol decision, so it is always 0x04 there.
# Valgrind matches suppressions against *mangled* names: an outlined frame by
# its raw symbol, an inlined frame by its bare DWARF name (`to_bytes`) — not
# the demangled names its error log prints. The method name is
# length-prefixed in both the v0 and the legacy mangling: the symbol of
# `<P256Group as CpaceGroup>::to_bytes` ends in `...8to_bytes` (in v0,
# `...9P256Group...8to_bytes`), that of `scalar_to_bytes` in
# `...15scalar_to_bytes`. Until 0.6.1 the glob was
# `*P256Group*to_bytes*`, which matched both, and so also suppressed
# `scalar_to_bytes`, run on the tainted SPAKE2 `w` and SPAKE2+ `w0`;
# `*P256Group*8to_bytes*` matches `to_bytes` alone — checked against the
# symbols of a debug build and of a release build with debug info, where
# `to_bytes` is an outlined function. The leading `...` still suppresses
# everything beneath the call (within `--num-callers`), the affine conversion
# (a field inversion) included; the same conversion also runs unsuppressed on
# secret points in every compressed encoding (below), so a leak in it shows
# there too, unless inlining compiles it differently at the two sites.
# Requiring a sec1 frame as well would be narrower, but release inlining can
# fold sec1's dispatch into this frame, as it folds the hash-to-curve
# internals in Family 1. That waits for the frames, as a CI run with
# `--gen-suppressions=all` prints them (mangled, as matched).
#
# Compressed encodings are no longer suppressed at all. Until 0.6.1,
# `oprf_p256::point_to_bytes` went through sec1's compressed form, whose
# `compress_y` branches on the parity of y; three entries here suppressed it
# on the stated ground that only public, wire-bound elements reached it.
# That ground was false — the unblinded OPRF value and the P256Dh shared
# point went through it too. Taint from those two never reached it, because
# their secret scalars were parsed from laundered copies; what the three
# entries did absorb was the blinded element, tainted through the password
# and public by design. Both are fixed: every compressed P-256 encoding in
# pakery-crypto now goes through a constant-time encoder, and the scalars
# stay tainted, so a regression there fails this harness instead of being
# suppressed.
{
   p256-sec1-tag-dispatch-group-to-bytes-cond
   Memcheck:Cond
   ...
   fun:*P256Group*8to_bytes*
}
{
   p256-sec1-tag-dispatch-group-to-bytes-value8
   Memcheck:Value8
   ...
   fun:*P256Group*8to_bytes*
}
{
   p256-sec1-tag-dispatch-group-to-bytes-value4
   Memcheck:Value4
   ...
   fun:*P256Group*8to_bytes*
}
